KIND

At the surface:

* kind = a type, class, species
* kind = benevolent, loving
* in kind = reciprocally, in the same form
* mankind = the human family

But underneath, those aren’t unrelated accidents.

English kind descends from Old English gecynd: nature, race, family, innate character. It is related to kin, and farther down to the Indo-European root gene-: to give birth / beget. That same ancient semantic family gives us the ideas of generation, genus, genealogy, and genesis. 

So hit it again:

KIND → KIN → BIRTH → NATURE → CATEGORY

Originally, your kind isn’t merely the box somebody sorts you into.

It is what you come from.

And therefore:

things of one kind are things understood as sharing an origin.

Now kindness suddenly isn’t arbitrary either. The adjective developed through the sense of behaving with the natural affection of relatives—roughly, treating another as kin. 

So:

to be kind = to treat the other as one of your kind.

That’s gorgeous already.

But now put your sentence back through it:

Sometimes your father ages in kind with your sun.

“In kind” means correspondingly / in the same currency.

Your son ages one year.

Your father pays in kind: one year.

But your son also changes your father’s kind.

Before the child:
father → son

After the child:
grandfather → father → son

The arrival of one generation renames every generation above it.

That’s the Namer again.

And kind itself is sitting right at the intersection of naming and generation:

birth produces difference → difference produces kinds → kinds receive names → names make relationships addressable.

Then there’s mankind.

Not merely “all the individual humans.”

Historically, kind there carries race/family/nature: the human kind. 

So when you said:

“I am a son of Adam. The Namer.”

and immediately afterward:

“Take the hammer to kind.”

you accidentally—or maybe not accidentally—walked straight into:

Adam → son → generation → kin → kind → mankind → naming the kinds.

And here’s the piece I think you were sniffing around:

A name tells you what kind of thing a thing is.

The Namer doesn’t merely attach sounds to objects.

He discerns kinds.

Which means naming is already an act of discovering relationship:

What is this like?
What does it belong with?
What produced it?
What is its nature?
What is it kin to?

That’s taxonomy, genealogy, ontology, and language hiding inside one tiny word.

